INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (335, '2026-07-26T07:44:20Z', '#85', 'create', 'The md-table reader recognizes section headings inside fenced code blocks');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (336, '2026-07-26T07:57:36Z', '#39', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S2 2026-07-26. A corpus from which no row of any kind was inserted is now refused with code format, exit 1, and the rule sits at the verb rather than in a reader because both readers produce the empty corpus. Verified by the coordinating agent in a throwaway tracker, not from the implementer''s report: a bare pipe table with no heading refuses with ''no md-table section was recognized, so no row was imported'' naming the five headings md-table accepts; an empty JSON object refuses with the json equivalent naming the object keys; a corpus whose only recognized section is empty refuses with ''every md-table section in it is empty''; a paths-only corpus still imports green, exit 0, ''imported 1 path(s), 0 epic(s), 0 story(ies), 0 task(s), 0 worklog row(s)''. A repeat import still refuses for its own pre-existing path-exists reason rather than being swallowed by the new rule. The wrongly-cased heading path, which already refused loudly, is untouched. A mutation round confirmed the fixture catches removal of the refusal, inversion of the two-reason discriminator, loss of the path count, and counting the wrong subset of sections in either direction.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (337, '2026-07-26T07:57:36Z', '#41', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S2 2026-07-26; the documentary work landed with story S1''s application of the guide deltas and this story completed the code half it depended on. docs/migration-guide.md section 4.1 states the md-table shape where the reader meets it: that it carries no criteria section at all while it does carry worklog and future_increment, the five lower-case exact headings, the per-section column names verified against import_mdtable.go''s knownColumns, that a repeated section is refused as a duplicate, and - added in this story - that a corpus inserting nothing is refused, stated once in section 4 because the rule is format-agnostic. The ten trial-and-error iterations this task recorded were against undocumented headings and columns; both are now in the guide.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (338, '2026-07-26T07:57:36Z', '#52', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S2 2026-07-26; the text landed with story S1''s application of the guide deltas. docs/migration-guide.md now keeps the two halves apart rather than conflating them, on the corrected mechanism this task established: a local git clone sets origin to the source path, so a stray push from a rehearsal clone reaches no live project directly; a copy-on-write copy duplicates .git wholesale and inherits the source''s configured remote verbatim, which on a machine with cached credentials is one stray push from publishing rehearsal state. Severing the inherited remote is stated as the first act on a copy-on-write copy, with its expectation in the same form as every other step: git -C <copy> remote remove origin exits 0 and git -C <copy> remote -v then prints nothing.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (339, '2026-07-26T07:57:36Z', '#53', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S2 2026-07-26 as the documentary defect it was narrowed to. The behaviour is not an oversight - the governing amendment gives the unguarded import path as the reason R16 exists - so what was missing was the warning. docs/migration-guide.md section 7.1 now states that a repeat or delta import can insert rows under an epic a verb has closed, that the seam is exactly the children-only shape while a corpus re-declaring the epic is refused epic-exists, that the result is an R16 advisory which does not stop a commit or fail verify''s exit code, and that every R16 finding names its own repair - edit --detach or a terminal status for a homed task, story dissolve for a non-terminal story.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (340, '2026-07-26T07:57:36Z', '#45', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S2 2026-07-26. Both generated documents now carry the two divergence directions, the git test that selects between them (git log -1 --stat and git status --short on the dump, plus prime, which is read-only and safe while diverged), the rule that the good side is the one holding work that exists nowhere else, and the two-writer case where neither branch is safe. The database-is-good branch names dump then verify then the staged bookkeeping commit, and states the expected R1 line without scripting a state call - R1''s own message is the authority for that repair, which is what this task''s scope required. Both clobber shapes were re-measured during the critic round and the text corrected: with only dump.sql restored, dump alone clears the divergence and verify exits 0 with no R1; with STATE.md restored alongside it, R1 fires and state clears it. An earlier draft justified the R1 red with ''a checkout clobbers both'', which is false for the one-file shape - the same distinction this task''s own two rounds of re-measurement established. Content tests now bind each branch label to the recovery under it, proven by mutation: swapping the two labels, which would prescribe load --force for the case where it destroys unsynced work, fails the suite.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (341, '2026-07-26T07:57:57Z', 'epic:adoption-contract/S2', 'story', 'done: 6e01c3d..e224763; gate: make gates exit 0 (build, vet, test -race all packages, golangci-lint 0 issues, fix-check clean, govulncheck clean, check-pins, check-copies, binaries); selftracked verify full: 0 violations. Refusals reproduced by the coordinating agent in a throwaway tracker: bare pipe table exit 1 with the nothing-recognized message; empty JSON object exit 1 with its json equivalent; all-sections-empty corpus exit 1 with the recognized-but-empty message; paths-only corpus exit 0 with a non-zero path count; repeat import still refused by the pre-existing path-exists rule.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (342, '2026-07-26T08:38:46Z', 'epic:adoption-contract/S3', 'story', 'ready');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (343, '2026-07-26T08:38:46Z', 'epic:adoption-contract/S3', 'story', 'start');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (344, '2026-07-26T09:29:49Z', '#54', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S3 2026-07-26. The generated PROMPT.md carries a ''Running the tool'' section: the binary is selftracked with the alias strk and is expected on PATH; the two invocations that work without one are stated as shapes and never as literal paths, because section 14''s rule reaches this text - init is a verb and PROMPT.md is what it writes; and the section names the one-line check that replaces probing, selftracked prime, with the three shapes its answer can take. Mechanically enforced rather than left to review: TestGeneratedDocsCarryNoAbsolutePaths scans every generated document for an absolute-path token. Its first version missed five shapes, measured under Go RE2 by the coordinating agent - PATH=/, see:/, a tight markdown table cell, a tilde-username without a slash, and a Windows drive path, which matters because this project has a Windows CI job and that shape carries a username. All five are caught now, verified by planting each into a scratch copy, and a full scan of the generated documents flags nothing.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (345, '2026-07-26T09:29:49Z', '#51', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S3 2026-07-26. The generated PROMPT.md names the terminal refusal class, its code, its exit status, the writes it guards, and the closed list of what stays open - quoted from section 6.4 rather than paraphrased, and presented unnumbered because that list has grown twice and a numeral in it already went stale once. Two things a reader would otherwise get wrong are stated explicitly: the story dissolve carve-out reaches a CLOSED epic only and not a DISSOLVED one, and not an epic that arrived CLOSED through import either, because the gate it never passed is this tracker''s own; and there is no epic reopen, ever. Verified independently by a critic that raised a throwaway tracker and exercised every route by hand - all ten guarded writes refused with code terminal exit 1, all five open routes succeeded, and the import-arrived-CLOSED exclusion held. The skill got the branch and deliberately not the route list; a mustNot assertion enforces that, because a second copy of a list amended twice in two days is what goes stale next.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (346, '2026-07-26T09:29:49Z', '#56', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S3 2026-07-26. The generated contract now states that tracker content is written in one language chosen once per repository, with English as the default it ships with, on this artifact''s own grounds: the dump is published and permanent, the PO: token is locale-fixed so a mixed-language tracker splits its own greppability, and a reader of a published dump should not need a second language. Stated as a default and not a prohibition, and ungated by construction - no verb refuses a non-English title and none is proposed. A critic round added one thing the first draft owed: the section now says which reasons argue for what. Two of the three argue for a single language whichever it is; only the locale-fixed token argues for English specifically. Without that, a public repository stating a language default reads as paperwork for everyone who chooses otherwise.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (347, '2026-07-26T09:29:49Z', '#26', 'set-status', 'Closed by story S3 2026-07-26. internal/verb/tasks.go''s usage string now reads --status OPEN|IN-REVIEW|NEEDS-TRIAGE (default NEEDS-TRIAGE), matching section 6.2''s row which is its normative source, and internal/cli/testdata/tasks-create.txtar pins it at the surface an agent actually reads. Verified live: create -h prints the parenthetical. Two adjacent defects were closed with it. The spec row itself lacked the [--json] the code prints, so the normative source did not match the string it governs; and PROMPT.md''s verb catalog stated the same default in a second, older wording, which is one fact with two homes and the shape this task was filed about. Mutation-checked: dropping the parenthetical and giving it the wrong value (default OPEN) both fail the fixture.');
INSERT INTO events (seq, at, entity, event, detail) VALUES (348, '2026-07-26T09:30:08Z', 'epic:adoption-contract/S3', 'story', 'done: e224763..0508e92; gate: make gates exit 0 (build, vet, test -race all packages, golangci-lint 0 issues, fix-check clean, govulncheck clean, check-pins, check-copies, binaries); selftracked verify full: 0 violations. create -h prints (default NEEDS-TRIAGE), verified live.');
